wser helper (${Uce}). The cause is how this session was started, or a setting for MCP servers. To see which, start claude with --debug, run /claude-test, and read the line about $.mcp.connect in the debug log (in ~/.claude/debug). If the setting is your organization's, only your administrator can change it.`,Ln="Claude Test did not start: its browser helper did not come up. Run /claude-test. If the helper was only slow to start, that will work. If it keeps failing, check that node is installed, and see /mcp for the error; you can reconnect the helper there, then run /claude-test.",Ht="Claude Test did not start: Claude Code would not connect its browser helper in this session. Restart Claude Code and run /claude-test. If that fails too, start claude with --debug, run /claude-test, and read the line about $.mcp.connect in the debug log (in ~/.claude/debug); it says why.",On="Claude Test did not start: another MCP server in this session already runs Claude Test's browser helper. In /mcp, switch off the server whose Command ends in playwright-mcp.mjs, then run /claude-test.",kn="Starting the Claude Test browser helper.",Nn="Claude Test did not start its browser helper: this build of Claude Code cannot check its files, so it starts nothing. Please report this.";function In(e,n,r){switch(n){case"differs":return`Claude Test did not start its browser helper: ${r??"a file"} in ${e} is not what this version of Claude Code ships. Delete ${e} and run /claude-test; the next run writes it afresh.`;case"linked":return`Claude Test did not start its browser helper: ${r??e} is a symbolic link, and it only runs from real folders. Delete that link (not what it points at) and run /claude-test; the next run writes the folder afresh.`;case"unwritable":return`Claude Test did not start its browser helper: it could not write its files under ${e}. Check that the folder can be written, or delete it, then run /claude-test.`;case"diskless":return"Claude Test's browser helper can't run in this se